{"task_id": "Mbpp/74", "solution": "import collections\nclass PatternMatcher:\n    \"\"\"\n    Encapsulates the core logic for comparing string and pattern lists and\n    managing consistent one-to-one mappings.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._pattern_to_string_map = {}\n        self._string_to_pattern_map = {}\n\n    def compare(self, string_list, pattern_list):\n        \"\"\"\n        Performs the comparison logic based on the defined business rules\n        and returns True for a match, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        if len(string_list) != len(pattern_list):\n            return False\n\n        for s_elem, p_elem in zip(string_list, pattern_list):\n            # Check pattern_to_string_map\n            if p_elem in self._pattern_to_string_map:\n                if self._pattern_to_string_map[p_elem] != s_elem:\n                    return False\n            else:\n                self._pattern_to_string_map[p_elem] = s_elem\n\n            # Check string_to_pattern_map\n            if s_elem in self._string_to_pattern_map:\n                if self._string_to_pattern_map[s_elem] != p_elem:\n                    return False\n            else:\n                self._string_to_pattern_map[s_elem] = p_elem\n\n        return True\ndef is_samepatterns(string_list, pattern_list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Provides the public API for checking if a string list follows a given pattern list.\n    This function instantiates PatternMatcher and calls its compare method.\n    \"\"\"\n    matcher = PatternMatcher()\n    return matcher.compare(string_list, pattern_list)"}
