{"task_id": "Mbpp/120", "solution": "def max_product_tuple(input_list: list[tuple]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum absolute product between numbers in pairs of tuples.\n    Returns the maximum absolute product found, or 0 if the list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_abs_product = 0\n\n    for item in input_list:\n        # Assuming valid tuples with two numerical elements based on design context.\n        element1, element2 = item\n        current_product = element1 * element2\n        current_abs_product = abs(current_product)\n\n        if current_abs_product > max_abs_product:\n            max_abs_product = current_abs_product\n\n    return max_abs_product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "def search(arr: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the element that appears only once in a sorted array.\n    Uses binary search to efficiently locate the unique element by adjusting search range based on paired elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    low = 0\n    high = len(arr) - 1\n\n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n\n        # Ensure mid is even for consistent pair checking (arr[mid] with arr[mid+1]).\n        if mid % 2 == 1:\n            mid -= 1\n\n        # If arr[mid] and arr[mid+1] form a pair, the unique element is to the right.\n        if mid + 1 < len(arr) and arr[mid] == arr[mid+1]:\n            low = mid + 2\n        # Otherwise, the unique element is at or to the left of arr[mid].\n        else:\n            high = mid - 1\n\n    # After the loop, 'low' will be the index of the unique element.\n    return arr[low]"}
