{"task_id": "Mbpp/140", "solution": "def extract_singly(input_list: list[iter]) -> set[int | float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Flattens a list of iterables into a single set of unique numeric elements.\n\n    Args:\n        input_list: A list where each element is an iterable (e.g., list, tuple).\n\n    Returns:\n        A set containing all unique numeric elements extracted from the nested iterables.\n    \"\"\"\n    unique_numbers = set()\n    for iterable_item in input_list:\n        for element in iterable_item:\n            if isinstance(element, (int, float)):\n                unique_numbers.add(element)\n    return unique_numbers"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/141", "solution": "def pancake_sort(input_list: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Sorts a list of comparable elements in ascending order using the pancake sort algorithm.\"\"\"\n    arr = list(input_list) # Create a mutable copy to perform flips\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # Helper function to reverse a sublist from index 0 to k (inclusive)\n    def _flip(sub_arr, k):\n        start = 0\n        while start < k:\n            sub_arr[start], sub_arr[k] = sub_arr[k], sub_arr[start]\n            start += 1\n            k -= 1\n\n    # One by one, reduce the current size of the array and find the maximum element\n    for curr_size in range(n, 1, -1):\n        # Find the index of the maximum element in the current sublist (0 to curr_size-1)\n        max_idx = 0\n        for i in range(1, curr_size):\n            if arr[i] > arr[max_idx]:\n                max_idx = i\n\n        # If the maximum element is not already at its correct sorted position\n        if max_idx != curr_size - 1:\n            # Move the maximum element to the beginning by flipping the sublist up to max_idx\n            _flip(arr, max_idx)\n\n            # Move the maximum element to its correct final position by flipping the entire current sublist\n            _flip(arr, curr_size - 1)\n            \n    return arr"}
