{"task_id": "Mbpp/170", "solution": "def sum_range_list(numbers: list[int], start_index: int, end_index: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of numbers in a list within a specified inclusive range.\n    Returns 0 if the list is empty or the effective range (after index adjustment) is invalid.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n\n    # BR5: If the list is empty, the sum shall be 0, regardless of the indices.\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # BR4: If the start index is greater than the end index, the system shall consider the range invalid and return 0.\n    if start_index > end_index:\n        return 0\n\n    # BR3: Adjust indices to be within the list bounds [0, n-1].\n    # Python slicing handles out-of-bounds indices somewhat, but explicit clamping ensures adherence to BR3's spirit\n    # and consistency with subsequent checks.\n    effective_start = max(0, start_index)\n    effective_end = min(n - 1, end_index)\n\n    # After clamping, if the effective start index is greater than the effective end index,\n    # it means the effective range is empty or invalid (e.g., initial range completely outside list).\n    if effective_start > effective_end:\n        return 0\n    \n    # BR1, BR2: Calculate the sum of elements within the adjusted inclusive range.\n    # Python list slicing is [start:end_exclusive]. To include effective_end, we use effective_end + 1.\n    return sum(numbers[effective_start : effective_end + 1])"}
