{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "_memo = {}\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of possible sequences of length n,\n    such that each element is a positive integer and is greater than\n    or equal to twice the previous element but less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    global _memo\n    _memo.clear()  # Clear memoization cache for each new calculation.\n\n    total_sequences = 0\n    # Iterate through all possible positive integers for the first element.\n    for first_element in range(1, m + 1):\n        # Recursively count valid sequences starting with 'first_element'.\n        total_sequences += _count_recursive(1, first_element, m, n)\n    return total_sequences\ndef _count_recursive(current_length: int, last_element: int, m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Internal recursive helper function with memoization to count sequences\n    based on the current length and the last element added.\n    \"\"\"\n    if (current_length, last_element) in _memo:\n        return _memo[(current_length, last_element)]\n\n    # Base case: A sequence of desired length 'n' has been formed.\n    if current_length == n:\n        return 1\n\n    count = 0\n    # Iterate through possible values for the next element, adhering to the rules.\n    for next_element in range(2 * last_element, m + 1):\n        count += _count_recursive(current_length + 1, next_element, m, n)\n\n    _memo[(current_length, last_element)] = count\n    return count"}
