{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    # If all bits are already set (e.g., for 0, or a power of 2 minus 1),\n    # or if n is 0, find the bit length needed.\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n    \n    # Determine the number of bits required to represent n.\n    # We look for the most significant bit that is 0.\n    num_bits = n.bit_length()\n\n    # Iterate from the most significant bit down to the least significant bit\n    # to find the first unset bit (0).\n    for i in range(num_bits - 1, -1, -1):\n        # Check if the i-th bit is unset (0).\n        if not (n & (1 << i)):\n            # If unset, set this bit and return the new number.\n            return n | (1 << i)\n    \n    # If no unset bit is found (i.e., all bits are already set up to num_bits),\n    # this means n is of the form 2^k - 1. We then set the next higher bit.\n    return n | (1 << num_bits)"}
