{"task_id": "Mbpp/462", "solution": "def combinations_list(input_list: list) -> list[list]:\n    \"\"\"Generates all possible combinations of elements from the given list.\n\n    Includes an empty list and follows the specific order from the SRS example.\n    \"\"\"\n    combinations = [[]]\n    for element in input_list:\n        # Create new combinations by appending the current element to existing ones.\n        new_combinations = []\n        for combo in combinations:\n            new_combinations.append(combo + [element])\n        combinations.extend(new_combinations)\n    return combinations"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/463", "solution": "def max_subarray_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product of a contiguous subarray within the given array.\n    Returns 0 if the input array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    max_so_far = arr[0]\n    min_so_far = arr[0]\n    overall_max_product = arr[0]\n\n    for i in range(1, len(arr)):\n        num = arr[i]\n\n        # If the current number is negative, we swap max_so_far and min_so_far.\n        # This is because multiplying a negative number with min_so_far might yield a new maximum.\n        if num < 0:\n            max_so_far, min_so_far = min_so_far, max_so_far\n\n        # Update max_so_far and min_so_far for the current position.\n        max_so_far = max(num, max_so_far * num)\n        min_so_far = min(num, min_so_far * num)\n\n        # The overall maximum product is the maximum of the current overall_max_product and max_so_far.\n        overall_max_product = max(overall_max_product, max_so_far)\n\n    return overall_max_product"}
